// expect-exit: 94
//
// `Vec<T>` — OUT OF RANGE: vec_get at a NEGATIVE index -- the half a C habit leaves out.
//
// `expect-exit: 94` is this file's `OutOfBounds` arm, so REACHING FAILSAFE
// THROUGH IT is the assertion. Every other path out of `main` exits with a code
// that names its own line, so a wrong answer is never 94.
//
// ONE CASE PER FILE, because a trapping call cannot be followed by an assertion
// in the same program — the split `probe08b` and `probe08c` take, for the same
// reason.
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS MORE HERE THAN IN AN ORDINARY CONTAINER. `Vec<T>.items` is a
// `wild T->`, a bare pointer, and D-070's bounds guard is not emitted for it
// (`SAFETY.md` §5.3, S-23, RX-111). Without the check inside the accessor this
// program would exit 0 having read or written unrelated heap memory. The trap
// below is this library's, not the language's — and the point of the file is
// that it happens at all.
mod:vec_oob_get_negative;

use "../../src/core/vec.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:argv) {
    // Derived from `argv` so nothing folds: `argv.len` is 1 with no arguments.
    int64:past = argv.len;

    Vec<int64>:v = raw vec_init::<int64>(4i64);
    drop vec_push(@v, 11i64);
    // An index derived from an `int32` field can be negative, `i < count`
    // accepts it, and a negative index reads BACKWARDS off the block. Every
    // accessor checks `0 <= i` as well (`SAFETY.md` S-23).
    discard(raw vec_get(v, 0i64 - past));

    // Unreachable. If the accessor ever stops checking, this is the exit that
    // says so — and it says it as a DIFFERENT code from the trap, so "the check
    // was removed" and "the check fired" can never be confused.
    drop vec_free(@v);
    exit 50i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
